<p>Spanish authorities prepared a controlled evacuation of more than 140 passengers and crew from the Dutch-flagged MV Hondius, a cruise ship scheduled to arrive off Tenerife on Sunday, May 10, after a hantavirus outbreak linked to the vessel killed three people and sickened others. The World Health Organization said six of eight reported cases had been laboratory-confirmed as hantavirus infections. All six were identified as Andes virus, a strain with documented limited person-to-person transmission among close contacts. Spain’s plan calls for passengers to move through an isolated, cordoned route from the ship to Tenerife South airport, with no contact with the local population, according to Spanish emergency officials and Canary Islands authorities. WHO Director-General Tedros Adhanom Ghebreyesus was due in Tenerife on Saturday to help coordinate the operation, while the United States arranged a medical repatriation flight for 17 Americans, who were expected to go to Nebraska for isolation. The WHO assessed the risk to the global population as low. Health authorities continued contact tracing for people who had left the ship before the outbreak was identified. The ship operator said no one remaining on board had symptoms, and the vessel was expected to arrive at Tenerife on Sunday.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/cMpgDdLY-UJBdPuRUv7HuFMPJNhcH9GMDgeyBG6bZ6AR1XY_3kg_NLGZ_FQcmFXpMRklt6B8w57q0nUVfRGxRGetIsknYaBQlE_DvP2gob0P0tbktLk_Gy0wF20dbCPJYExy0rMeUyEA9hyEywcplJUmZNXw_PGnSULfN56p9Alo7qYIYbYtvaTTDtAcyFfpImk1KUbsqt6HKwrt5mg' alt='Passengers on the hantavirus-stricken cruise ship MV Hondius watch epidemiologists board the boat in Praia during the voyage to Tenerife.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Weather window: Canary Islands authorities warned that the evacuation might need to be completed within 24 hours of arrival because worsening weather could delay operations for days or weeks.</li><li>St. Helena gap: Reports said 28 people left the ship at the April 24 stop before the first death had been identified as part of a hantavirus outbreak, leaving health agencies to reconstruct their later movements.</li><li>Current patients: The WHO said four patients were hospitalized: one in intensive care in Johannesburg, two in the Netherlands and one in Zurich.</li><li>Spanish passengers: Spanish reports said the 14 Spaniards on the voyage would be flown to Madrid and isolated at the Gómez Ulla military hospital.</li></ul><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>Passengers on board: Some Spanish passengers said they feared stigma or compulsory isolation after returning home more than they feared immediate illness; others expressed resignation about the delayed return. (<a href='https://www.newsday.com/news/nation/hantavirus-cruise-spain-r14793'>Newsday</a>)</li><li>Tenerife residents: Some residents expressed anger and concern about the ship’s arrival, and protests in Tenerife opposed receiving the vessel despite official assurances about isolation measures. (<a href='https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/c5y0gkg038yo'>BBC</a>)</li><li>Tierra del Fuego officials: Officials in Argentina’s tourism-dependent southern province rejected suggestions that the outbreak originated there and urged investigators to examine other locations visited before boarding. (<a href='https://abcnews.com/International/wireStory/argentinas-hot-spot-antarctic-cruises-insists-cause-hantavirus-132798777'>ABC News</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://www.aljazeera.com/video/newsfeed/2026/5/8/protests-in-the-canary-islands-as-virus-stricken-ship-heads-for-port'>Protests in the Canary Islands as virus-stricken ship heads for port</a> - aljazeera.com</li><li><a href='https://japantoday.com/category/world/spain-readies-for-evacuations-as-a-hantavirus-hit-cruise-ship-heads-for-canary-islands'>Spain readies for evacuations as hantavirus-hit cruise ship heads for the Canary Islands</a> - japantoday.com</li><li><a href='https://www.straitstimes.com/world/w